<h2>Vaults</h2>
<p>
  A vault is any folder. Its <code>.md</code> files are the notes, and its subfolders are their
  folders. Nothing is added to the folder: no database, and no hidden settings. Names that start
  with a dot, such as <code>.git</code> or <code>.obsidian</code>, are left out. Switch vaults from
  the button at the left of the toolbar, or with File, Switch Vault. Preferences lists your vaults.
  Forget takes one off the list and leaves the folder alone.
</p>
<pre><code>cargo build -p rivet-notes
./target/debug/rivet-notes [VAULT]
./target/debug/rivet-notes tags [VAULT]</code></pre>
<p>
  From a release build of the workspace, the same commands are
  <code>cargo build --release -p rivet-notes</code> and
  <code>./target/release/rivet-notes [VAULT]</code>.
</p>

<h2>What the first version does</h2>
<dl class="features">
  <dt>Properties</dt>
  <dd>
    The <code>---</code> block at the top of a note is shown as properties: text, number, checkbox,
    date, list, tags, or link. The file stays plain YAML. Saving writes back only what changed in
    that block.
  </dd>
  <dt>Browse</dt>
  <dd>
    By folder, in a tree that folds, or by tag. Search looks at titles, paths, the first line, and
    tags. <code>#name</code> searches tags only.
  </dd>
  <dt>Links</dt>
  <dd>
    <code>[[Note name]]</code>, with optional shown text or a heading. Typing <code>[[</code> lists
    matches. Peek lists backlinks. Renaming a note updates the links that point at it.
  </dd>
  <dt>Quick Open and Quick Actions</dt>
  <dd>
    Ctrl+P finds a note from part of its title or folder. Shift+Ctrl+P runs a command or option by
    name. They use the same panel as Rivet Files.
  </dd>
  <dt>Editing</dt>
  <dd>
    Source mode shows the Markdown as written. Live mode still writes Markdown, and renders every
    line except the one you are on. Ctrl+Space opens a reading view. Notes save a second after you
    stop typing, and with Ctrl+S.
  </dd>
  <dt>Daily notes</dt>
  <dd>
    Optional, in Preferences, Modules. Ctrl+D opens today's note, named by its date, in a folder you
    pick.
  </dd>
  <dt>Peek</dt>
  <dd>
    F11 opens a panel about the open note: width, file details, dates, and properties.
  </dd>
</dl>

